{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013U2Xis2hQM5f8PNUmpErsG","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Challenger space shuttle accident date 1986\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Space Shuttle Challenger disaster\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Shuttle_Challenger_disaster\"},{\"title\":\"Challenger Accident | History | Research Starters | EBSCOhost\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Explosion: Date, Crew & Shuttle Program | HISTORY\",\"url\":\"<redacted> disaster | Summary, Date, Cause, & Facts | Britannica\",\"url\":\"<redacted> space shuttle Challenger explodes after liftoff | January 28, 1986 | HISTORY\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Space Shuttle Disaster: Jan. 28, 1986\",\"url\":\"<redacted> anniversary lessons from the NASA Challenger disaster : NPR\",\"url\":\"<redacted> video reveals 86 challenger disaster\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Searches\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Space Shuttle Challenger broke apart on January 28, 1986, 73 seconds into its flight, killing all seven crew members. The spacecraft disintegrated about 46,000 feet (14 km) above the Atlantic Ocean, off the coast of Cape Canaveral, Florida, at 16:39:13 UTC (11:39:13 a.m. EST).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"524ad19d-84e8-441c-90e0-58573e8f9701","uuid":"43ea351a-bc01-45b9-86a1-4539431db1ad","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:11:46.876Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Challenger space shuttle accident date 1986","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01UtBYh53T1Lc1NDPXUrDX1h","content":[{"title":"Space Shuttle Challenger disaster","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Shuttle_Challenger_disaster"},{"title":"Challenger Accident | History | Research Starters | EBSCOhost","url":"<redacted> Explosion: Date, Crew & Shuttle Program | HISTORY","url":"<redacted> disaster | Summary, Date, Cause, & Facts | Britannica","url":"<redacted> space shuttle Challenger explodes after liftoff | January 28, 1986 | HISTORY","url":"<redacted> Space Shuttle Disaster: Jan. 28, 1986","url":"<redacted> anniversary lessons from the NASA Challenger disaster : NPR","url":"<redacted> video reveals 86 challenger disaster","url":"<redacted> Searches","url":"<redacted> Space Shuttle Challenger broke apart on January 28, 1986, 73 seconds into its flight, killing all seven crew members. The spacecraft disintegrated about 46,000 feet (14 km) above the Atlantic Ocean, off the coast of Cape Canaveral, Florida, at 16:39:13 UTC (11:39:13 a.m. EST)."],"durationSeconds":3.176347757000001,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014KziHeGEyN3sQrbahhNHBn","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Voyager 2 Uranus encounter date 1986\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Voyager Uranus Science Summary\",\"url\":\"<redacted> of History - 40th Anniversary of Voyager 2’s Uranus Encounter | NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL)\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2 PPS Uranus Encounter Period - Voyager 2 Interstellar Mission\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2 met Uranus 40 years ago\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2: First To Visit All Four Giants — NASA Science\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Years Ago: Voyager 2 Explores Uranus - NASA\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 1: URANUS ENCOUNTER\",\"url\":\"<redacted> on the search results, here's the information about Voyager 2's Uranus encounter:\n\nVoyager 2 met Uranus on January 24, 1986. More specifically, closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\n\nVoyager 2's encounter of Uranus began November 4, 1985 with an observatory phase, with activity building to a peak in late January 1986. The Uranus encounter officially came to an end on February 25, 1986.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"524ad19d-84e8-441c-90e0-58573e8f9701","uuid":"99dbe092-a1cc-4ade-83b1-e280190e9f15","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:11:47.726Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Voyager 2 Uranus encounter date 1986","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01WTUT12sNzcWnziCMHxWUZa","content":[{"title":"Voyager Uranus Science Summary","url":"<redacted> of History - 40th Anniversary of Voyager 2’s Uranus Encounter | NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL)","url":"<redacted> 2 PPS Uranus Encounter Period - Voyager 2 Interstellar Mission","url":"<redacted> 2 met Uranus 40 years ago","url":"<redacted> 2: First To Visit All Four Giants — NASA Science","url":"<redacted> Years Ago: Voyager 2 Explores Uranus - NASA","url":"<redacted> 1: URANUS ENCOUNTER","url":"<redacted> on the search results, here's the information about Voyager 2's Uranus encounter:\n\nVoyager 2 met Uranus on January 24, 1986. More specifically, closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\n\nVoyager 2's encounter of Uranus began November 4, 1985 with an observatory phase, with activity building to a peak in late January 1986. The Uranus encounter officially came to an end on February 25, 1986."],"durationSeconds":4.332862595999999,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EMZAJUxbyAvq8wqD8VYFjZ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Voyager 2 Uranus moons discovered names\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Voyager 2 - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_2\"},{\"title\":\"New moons of Uranus discovered by Voyager 2 | The Planetary Society\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2 met Uranus 40 years ago\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Uranus Science Summary\",\"url\":\"<redacted> (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puck_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Ophelia (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ophelia_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Cordelia (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cordelia_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Mysterious World: 30 Years Since Voyager 2’s Encounter With Uranus (Part 2) - AmericaSpace\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2 discovered ten moons when it flew past Uranus in 1986. Nearly 40 years later, James Webb found one it missed: Uranus's 29th known moon, a tiny world only about 10 kilometres wide hiding among the planet's inner moons.\",\"url\":\"<redacted> on the search results, here's what Voyager 2 discovered regarding Uranus's moons:\n\nVoyager 2 discovered 11 previously unknown moons: Cordelia, Ophelia, Bianca, Cressida, Desdemona, Juliet, Portia, Rosalind, Belinda, Puck and Perdita.\n\nThe closest approach to Uranus occurred on January 24, 1986, when Voyager 2 came within 81,500 km (50,600 mi) of the planet's cloudtops. Initially, scientists discovered 10 new moons in the data Voyager 2 sent back, and in 1999, additional analysis of Voyager 2 data revealed an 11th moon.\n\nNotably, all of the moons are named in honor of characters from the works of William Shakespeare and Alexander Pope.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"524ad19d-84e8-441c-90e0-58573e8f9701","uuid":"ccc9e6fd-97b5-4439-ab21-3e7fdce9ba85","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:11:48.411Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Voyager 2 Uranus moons discovered names","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Lh41ZCrrRh1qCKSXFaiqvs","content":[{"title":"Voyager 2 - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_2"},{"title":"New moons of Uranus discovered by Voyager 2 | The Planetary Society","url":"<redacted> 2 met Uranus 40 years ago","url":"<redacted> Uranus Science Summary","url":"<redacted> (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puck_(moon)"},{"title":"Ophelia (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ophelia_(moon)"},{"title":"Cordelia (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cordelia_(moon)"},{"title":"Mysterious World: 30 Years Since Voyager 2’s Encounter With Uranus (Part 2) - AmericaSpace","url":"<redacted> 2 discovered ten moons when it flew past Uranus in 1986. Nearly 40 years later, James Webb found one it missed: Uranus's 29th known moon, a tiny world only about 10 kilometres wide hiding among the planet's inner moons.","url":"<redacted> on the search results, here's what Voyager 2 discovered regarding Uranus's moons:\n\nVoyager 2 discovered 11 previously unknown moons: Cordelia, Ophelia, Bianca, Cressida, Desdemona, Juliet, Portia, Rosalind, Belinda, Puck and Perdita.\n\nThe closest approach to Uranus occurred on January 24, 1986, when Voyager 2 came within 81,500 km (50,600 mi) of the planet's cloudtops. Initially, scientists discovered 10 new moons in the data Voyager 2 sent back, and in 1999, additional analysis of Voyager 2 data revealed an 11th moon.\n\nNotably, all of the moons are named in honor of characters from the works of William Shakespeare and Alexander Pope."],"durationSeconds":4.813533566999999,"searchCount":1}}
