{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DRytaPHXgTvPZKQdDu8hFr","type":"tool_result","content":"# Voyager 2's Uranus Encounter\n\n**Closest Approach Distance:**\nVoyager 2 came within 50,640 miles (81,500 km) of Uranus' cloud tops on January 24, 1986.\n\n**Moons Discovered:**\nThe spacecraft initially revealed 10 new moons during this encounter. \"Initially, scientists discovered 10 new moons in the data Voyager 2 sent back, for a total of 15 moons known at the time.\" Among these was Puck, a small 100-mile diameter moon, and clearer images of Miranda, nicknamed the \"Frankenstein moon\" due to its unusual geological features.\n\n**Wind Speeds:**\nThe article does not contain information about wind speeds on Uranus. The content focuses on other atmospheric composition details, noting that \"Uranus' atmosphere is similar to that of the other gas giants, composed mainly of hydrogen and helium\" with underlying layers of water, ammonia, and methane ices, but specific wind speed measurements are not mentioned."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"524ad19d-84e8-441c-90e0-58573e8f9701","uuid":"1d832b57-f05d-4daa-8365-24f4dab98e39","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:11:58.445Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":136589,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Voyager 2's Uranus Encounter\n\n**Closest Approach Distance:**\nVoyager 2 came within 50,640 miles (81,500 km) of Uranus' cloud tops on January 24, 1986.\n\n**Moons Discovered:**\nThe spacecraft initially revealed 10 new moons during this encounter. \"Initially, scientists discovered 10 new moons in the data Voyager 2 sent back, for a total of 15 moons known at the time.\" Among these was Puck, a small 100-mile diameter moon, and clearer images of Miranda, nicknamed the \"Frankenstein moon\" due to its unusual geological features.\n\n**Wind Speeds:**\nThe article does not contain information about wind speeds on Uranus. The content focuses on other atmospheric composition details, noting that \"Uranus' atmosphere is similar to that of the other gas giants, composed mainly of hydrogen and helium\" with underlying layers of water, ammonia, and methane ices, but specific wind speed measurements are not mentioned.","durationMs":4199,"url":"<redacted>
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011nS9DFPeGJVM1mNoNmWNAg","type":"tool_result","content":"# Voyager 2's Uranus Encounter Summary\n\n## Mission Overview\nVoyager 2 conducted humanity's first close encounter with Uranus in January 1986, approaching within 81,500 kilometers of the planet's cloud tops on January 24. The spacecraft transmitted extensive scientific data about the distant ice giant and its system.\n\n## Key Discoveries\n\n**Moons**: Voyager confirmed five previously known large moons and detected 10 new ones, bringing the total to 15. The largest newly discovered moon, Puck, measures approximately 150 kilometers in diameter. The five large moons revealed diverse geological histories—Miranda particularly stood out as \"one of the strangest bodies yet observed in the solar system\" due to its massive fault canyons reaching 20 kilometers deep.\n\n**Rings**: The mission photographed all nine previously known rings and identified two new ones. The outermost epsilon ring consists primarily of ice boulders several feet across. Scientists theorize the ring system may be relatively young, possibly formed from a moon destroyed by impact.\n\n**Atmosphere & Conditions**: Uranus's blue-green hue results from methane absorbing red light. Wind speeds reached 40-160 meters per second at mid-latitudes, with equatorial winds reaching approximately 100 meters per second in the opposite direction.\n\n**Magnetic Field**: Uranus possesses an intense, highly tilted magnetic field offset 60 degrees from its rotation axis, creating a distinctive corkscrew-shaped magnetotail extending millions of kilometers behind the planet."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"524ad19d-84e8-441c-90e0-58573e8f9701","uuid":"0ae4ac5e-2e8d-4531-9578-b2f6b0055da9","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:12:01.108Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":19422,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Voyager 2's Uranus Encounter Summary\n\n## Mission Overview\nVoyager 2 conducted humanity's first close encounter with Uranus in January 1986, approaching within 81,500 kilometers of the planet's cloud tops on January 24. The spacecraft transmitted extensive scientific data about the distant ice giant and its system.\n\n## Key Discoveries\n\n**Moons**: Voyager confirmed five previously known large moons and detected 10 new ones, bringing the total to 15. The largest newly discovered moon, Puck, measures approximately 150 kilometers in diameter. The five large moons revealed diverse geological histories—Miranda particularly stood out as \"one of the strangest bodies yet observed in the solar system\" due to its massive fault canyons reaching 20 kilometers deep.\n\n**Rings**: The mission photographed all nine previously known rings and identified two new ones. The outermost epsilon ring consists primarily of ice boulders several feet across. Scientists theorize the ring system may be relatively young, possibly formed from a moon destroyed by impact.\n\n**Atmosphere & Conditions**: Uranus's blue-green hue results from methane absorbing red light. Wind speeds reached 40-160 meters per second at mid-latitudes, with equatorial winds reaching approximately 100 meters per second in the opposite direction.\n\n**Magnetic Field**: Uranus possesses an intense, highly tilted magnetic field offset 60 degrees from its rotation axis, creating a distinctive corkscrew-shaped magnetotail extending millions of kilometers behind the planet.","durationMs":6250,"url":"<redacted>
